  "query_preview": "..\n\nLong wavelength upper level ridging is now expanding across eastern \nNorth America and will hold position through the end of the week and \nwell into the weekend.  Plan view perspective of relative humidity \non isentropic surfaces supports a very subsaturated airmass \nthroughout much of the troposphere amidst deep and persistent \nsouthwesterly flow this week. Forecast soundings are telling with \nstrongly statically stable conditions from the surface through 18.0 \nkft agl. Virtually no precipitation potential exists for the next 5 \ndays.\n\nYet another long stretch of above normal temperatures with highs \ntoday and Wednesday in the 50s and highs during the end of the week \neclipsing 60 degrees for many areas. NBM probabilistic data suggests \na greater than 80% chance to reach 60 degrees Saturday for much of \nthe area. These readings will be some 15 to 20 degrees above normal. \nCould see some minor br or haze around each of the dawn to daybreak \nperiods with a lowered subsidence inversion into the surface. \nHowever, there is a persistent southwest gradient wind to contend \nwith and the ground has not really cooled off to this point relative \nto climatology given the recent above normal temperatures. Thus, \nmore of a period by period forecast with MOS guidance not overly \npessimistic the next 48 hours.\n\nModels have been consistent in advertising precipitation potential \nwith a deep trough system late Saturday and Sunday. All indications \npoint to a period of good to strong lift across the area as a cold \nfront tracks through area. Interesting note on the system is that \nensemble data suggests that temperatures will not be all that cold \nfollowing with forecasted surface temperature anomalies still \nsuggesting above normal daytime temperatures at day 7 in the lower \n40s. Will also offer that depending on the details of how the \nshortwave ejects through the area could be looking at breezy \npostfrontal conditions.",
